{
  "trace_id": "adaptive::q16",
  "architecture": "adaptive",
  "question": "This engine's chunking uses a specific target size and a specific overlap size that add up to a 20% overlap ratio \u2014 what are the two exact numbers, and why isn't cranking that ratio up simply a free way to guarantee boundary-spanning facts are retrievable?",
  "answer": "The engine\u2019s default chunking is set to  \n\n* **Target chunk size:**\u202f250\u202ftokens\u2003(`CHUNK_TARGET_TOKENS = 250`)  \n* **Overlap size:**\u202f50\u202ftokens\u2003(`CHUNK_OVERLAP_TOKENS = 50`)  \n\nThese values give a 20\u202f% overlap ratio\u202f\u2014\u202f50\u202f\u00f7\u202f250\u202f=\u202f0.20\u202f\u3010chunk-overlap-and-why-it-matters::0\u3011.  \n\nIncreasing the overlap ratio is not a free \u201cguarantee\u201d for retrieving boundary\u2011spanning facts because each extra overlapped token is duplicated in the vector store. That duplication inflates the index size, creates many near\u2011duplicate chunks, and causes those almost\u2011identical pieces to compete for the same top\u2011k retrieval slots. As a result, the corpus balloons and retrieval quality can actually degrade, with redundant hits crowding out other genuinely relevant material\u3010chunk-overlap-and-why-it-matters::0\u3011\u3010chunk-overlap-and-why-it-matters::1\u3011.",
